{% comment %}
  Selection logic only -- no layout, no styling. Drop this at the top of
  your markup and everything below it can use `pick`.

  It resolves the Themes and Eras settings against today.json:

    both chosen  -> a map that is one of those themes AND from one of
                    those eras, rotating a day at a time over the
                    combinations that exist
    one chosen   -> that dimension only
    none chosen  -> today's map from the whole pool

  The rotation and the picks both turn on the viewer's own local day, so
  both hold for their whole day and move on together at their midnight.

  Two things this is careful about, both found by running it rather than
  reading it: a settings keyname and a feed key of the same name collide
  (hence theme_options/era_options in the feed, themes/eras from the
  form), and `split` leaves a trailing empty string on some engines,
  which is why nothing here indexes straight into a split array.
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Settings live under trmnl.plugin_settings.custom_fields_values, keyed
  by keyname. A multi-select arrives as an array, a single choice as a
  string, and the stored value is derived from the option rather than
  equal to it -- "The 1700s" arrives as "the_1700s" -- which the feed's
  keys_by_label already accounts for.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign settings = trmnl.plugin_settings.custom_fields_values %}

{% assign chosen_themes = settings.themes %}
{% if chosen_themes == nil %}{% assign chosen_themes = themes %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_themes.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_themes = chosen_themes | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% assign chosen_eras = settings.eras %}
{% if chosen_eras == nil %}{% assign chosen_eras = eras %}{% endif %}
{% if chosen_eras.first == nil %}{% assign chosen_eras = chosen_eras | split: "," %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A boolean field arrives as the STRING "true" or "false", so it has to
  be compared, not just tested -- {% raw %}{% if show_description %}{% endraw %} is true even
  when the reader turned it off.

  An untouched field may also arrive as nothing at all, so the default
  matters: the caption and its context line are on unless switched off,
  and the credit is off unless switched on. A fresh install with nothing
  configured therefore shows a captioned map rather than a bare one.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign want_caption = true %}
{% if settings.show_caption == "false" %}{% assign want_caption = false %}{% endif %}

{% assign want_description = true %}
{% if settings.show_description == "false" %}{% assign want_description = false %}{% endif %}

{% assign want_credit = false %}
{% if settings.show_credit == "true" %}{% assign want_credit = true %}{% endif %}

{% comment %} Labels the person saw -> the keys this feed uses. {% endcomment %}
{% assign theme_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_themes %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign theme_keys = theme_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% assign era_keys = "" %}
{% for label in chosen_eras %}
  {% assign lbl = label | strip %}
  {% assign k = keys_by_label[lbl] %}
  {% if k %}{% assign era_keys = era_keys | append: k | append: "," %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% assign theme_keys = theme_keys | split: "," %}
{% assign era_keys = era_keys | split: "," %}

{% comment %} Count what survived, ignoring anything split left behind. {% endcomment %}
{% assign n_themes = 0 %}
{% for k in theme_keys %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n_themes = n_themes | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% assign n_eras = 0 %}
{% for k in era_keys %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n_eras = n_eras | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}

{% comment %} The keys worth rotating over. {% endcomment %}
{% assign wanted = "" %}
{% if n_themes > 0 and n_eras > 0 %}
  {% for t in theme_keys %}
    {% if t != "" %}
      {% for e in era_keys %}
        {% if e != "" %}
          {% capture cell %}{{ t }}__{{ e }}{% endcapture %}
          {% if cell_keys contains cell %}
            {% assign wanted = wanted | append: cell | append: "," %}
          {% endif %}
        {% endif %}
      {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
{% elsif n_themes > 0 %}
  {% for t in theme_keys %}{% if t != "" %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: t | append: "," %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% elsif n_eras > 0 %}
  {% for e in era_keys %}{% if e != "" %}{% assign wanted = wanted | append: e | append: "," %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  Fall back a step at a time: no surviving combination -> the chosen
  themes -> the whole pool. Somebody asking for 1700s railroad maps has
  asked for something that never existed, and still gets a railroad map.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign wanted = wanted | split: "," %}
{% assign n = 0 %}
{% for k in wanted %}{% if k != "" %}{% assign n = n | plus: 1 %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{% if n == 0 and n_themes > 0 %}
  {% assign wanted = theme_keys %}{% assign n = n_themes %}
{% endif %}
{% if n == 0 %}
  {% assign wanted = "all" | split: "," %}{% assign n = 1 %}
{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  Which day is it *here*? The long note further down explains why this is
  not the UTC day; it has to be worked out up here because the rotation
  over cells must turn over on the same tick the day's picks do. When they
  disagree -- and they did -- a viewer east of UTC spends the hours between
  their own midnight and midnight UTC on tomorrow's picks but yesterday's
  cell, which shows them a map they have already seen.

  A device with no usable clock gets `default_day`, the day the feed was
  built for, which is the old behaviour. Any real local day is a
  five-figure number, so that one comparison catches nil, 0 and garbage.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign local_seconds = trmnl.system.timestamp_utc | plus: trmnl.user.utc_offset %}
{% assign local_day = local_seconds | divided_by: 86400 %}
{% if local_day < 10000 %}{% assign local_day = default_day | plus: 0 %}{% endif %}

{% comment %} Today's key: the nth non-empty entry, n rotating by local day. {% endcomment %}
{% assign i = local_day | modulo: n %}
{% assign j = 0 %}
{% assign chosen_key = "all" %}
{% for k in wanted %}
  {% if k != "" %}
    {% if j == i %}{% assign chosen_key = k %}{% endif %}
    {% assign j = j | plus: 1 %}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% comment %}
  Which day is it *here*? Not in UTC -- here, on this device.

  The feed used to carry one day, chosen against the UTC date, so the
  map changed at the same instant everywhere. In Berlin that is 02:05,
  which reads as a new day. In Los Angeles it is 17:05 the previous
  afternoon; in Auckland it lands at midday and changes the map while
  somebody is looking at it.

  TRMNL hands us everything needed to do better: system.timestamp_utc is
  unix seconds, user.utc_offset is that viewer's offset in seconds, and
  the two added together and divided by 86400 is their own local day
  number -- the same integer daily.py counts in.

  The feed carries yesterday, today and tomorrow, which is all three
  values a device anywhere can land on while one file is live (offsets
  run -12 to +14, so 24 hours of file life spans 50 hours of local
  time). A missing or odd clock falls back to the day the feed was
  built for, which is the old behaviour.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign day_key = local_day | append: "" %}
{% assign day_picks = days[day_key] %}
{% if day_picks == nil %}{% assign day_picks = days[default_day] %}{% endif %}

{% assign pick = day_picks[chosen_key] %}
{% if pick == nil %}{% assign pick = day_picks["all"] %}{% endif %}

{% comment %}
  A pick is a list, not an object -- at 55 cells across three days,
  field names alone would cost about 20KB of the 95KB TRMNL allows.
  Unpack it once here so the layout below stays readable. The order is
  the feed's pick_fields.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign map_image_default = pick[0] %}
{% assign map_title = pick[1] %}
{% assign map_year = pick[2] %}
{% assign map_byline = pick[3] %}
{% assign map_description = pick[4] %}
{% assign map_place = pick[5] %}
{% assign map_category = pick[6] %}
{% assign map_item_id = pick[7] %}

{% comment %}
  The image, at the size this panel actually has.

  Compose ONLY from image_boxes. LOC renders a IIIF derivative on demand
  from a master that can be 11,000 pixels across, and the first request
  for a given size takes 12 to 18 seconds -- measured on the 1862 Little
  Falls view, !1872,1404 cold was 15.2s to first byte against 0.43s once
  cached. TRMNL's renderer gives up long before that and the panel comes
  up blank with the caption still on it. Every box in image_boxes has
  been warmed by the daily job, across all three days; any other box is
  one nobody has asked for, and a blank panel.

  Why not simply ship the largest and let small panels scale it down,
  which is what this did until the OG started coming up blank: the panel
  is 1-bit. Dithering discards everything finer than the panel's own
  grid, so pixels past it cannot sharpen anything -- they are decoded
  and dithered and thrown away. An OG shows 0.38 megapixels; the X-sized
  image is 2.10. That was 5.5x the work for an identical picture, and it
  was the difference between a render that fit the budget and one that
  did not.

  Column 0 is a finished URL, not a base, and this splits it at "/full/"
  to recover one. That is deliberate: markup already installed on a
  device reads column 0 straight into src, so shipping a bare base there
  would have pointed every one of them at a 302 to HTML -- a blank panel
  on every screen running the previous version. A feed may gain meaning;
  it may not change what a column already means.

  There is no multiplier between the two panels, which is why this reads
  from a table rather than doing arithmetic. The OG's CSS box is 800x480
  over an 800x480 panel; the X's is 1040x780 over a physical 1872x1404.
  A width matching neither takes image_box_default, the largest, which
  is never wrong -- only heavy.

  It asks for the colour scan (default.jpg) rather than the Library's
  greyscale one, because a colour panel can then show the colour and a
  monochrome one loses nothing: converting the colour file to luminance
  lands within 1/255 of the Library's own greyscale version, measured
  across a whole map.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign map_base = map_image_default | split: "/full/" | first %}
{% assign map_box = image_box_default %}
{% for panel in image_boxes %}
  {% if trmnl.device.width and trmnl.device.width <= panel.width %}
    {% assign map_box = panel.box %}
  {% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% assign map_image = map_base | append: "/full/!" | append: map_box | append: image_suffix %}
{% comment %}
  From here on:
    map_image         the map, at the size this panel has
    map_title         trimmed title
    map_year          four digits
    map_byline        "by Seward Porter in Bath, Me., 1837"
    map_description   a line of context, when the catalogue has one
    map_place         where it depicts
    map_category      "Railroads, 1850 - 1869"
    map_item_id       the LOC item number; append to item_url_prefix
    want_caption      whether to draw the caption block at all
    want_description  whether that block includes a line of context
    want_credit       whether to print the Library credit
{% endcomment %}
